<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chełm</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kholm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chełm is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the Ukrainian border. Founded as Kholm by Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in the 1230s, it became his capital and burial place, and it passed to Poland in the 14th century. It is known for the chalk tunnels beneath its old town and its cathedral hill.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chelm.jpg" alt="Chełm" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Akna · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Che%C5%82m,_Bazylika_NNMP.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chełm lies in the Lublin Voivodeship in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, south-east of Lublin and about 25 kilometres from the Ukrainian border. Its name is most often derived from the Proto-Slavic word for a hill, in reference to the fortified settlement on Wysoka Górka. Its landmarks are Castle Hill with the Baroque Basilica of the Birth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> and the Chełm Chalk Tunnels, which run for about 15 kilometres under the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement in the area dates from at least the 9th century, and in the 10th a fortified town served first as a centre of pagan worship. In 981 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> annexed it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1235 Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> granted the town a city charter, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed Halych in 1240 and 1241 he moved the capital of his domain to Chełm, where it stayed until 1272. He built a new castle on the hill in 1237, one of the few Ruthenian castles to withstand Mongol attacks, and set up an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> eparchy at the basilica. In 1366 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> of Poland took control of the region.</p><p>The city prospered in the 15th and 16th centuries, when Catholics, Orthodox Christians, Protestants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived there, and declined in the 17th through wars that ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After the Third Partition it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> awarded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Poles liberated the city from Austrian rule on 2 November 1918, nine days before Poland officially regained independence.</p><p>In September 1939 about 18,000 of the city's people, 60 per cent, were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. German forces occupied the city in October. On 1 December 1939 around 2,000 Jewish men were driven on a death march to Hrubieszów, and perhaps 400 survived. In 1942 the Jews of Chełm were deported to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobibor-extermination-camp">Sobibór</a> extermination camp or killed in the city, and only an estimated 60 survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chełm is a city of mostly industrial character with 60,231 inhabitants. From 1975 to 1998 it was the capital of its own voivodeship. Its Jewish past lives on in folklore: Chełm became the setting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> stories of foolish wise men, retold by writers such as Isaac Bashevis Singer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chełm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1912 the new <b>Chełm</b> Governorate was split off and attached to the Southwestern Krai to speed its russification.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded <b>Kholm</b> and Lviv, which he named after his son Leo, and welcomed German, Armenian and Jewish settlers to the towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a></li></ul></section>
